# Replace the forward plugin of the default server block.  The k3s Corefile
# imports this file before its own forward line.  Both lines accept all names,
# so CoreDNS uses this one, and the line from k3s never runs.
#
# The default server block sends each query to one of the three resolvers in
# the resolv.conf file on the node, and it makes that choice at random.  Two
# of the three resolvers are public.  Thus blocky receives one query in three.
# The block lists of blocky do not apply to the other two queries, and a name
# that blocky maps to an internal address gets the public address instead.
#
# `sequential` sends every query to blocky first.  CoreDNS uses the public
# resolvers only when blocky does not answer.
#
# The health check of the forward plugin sends a query for the root zone by
# default.  blocky gives a malformed answer to that query, and thus CoreDNS
# marks blocky as unavailable.  blocky answers correctly for tswn.us, so the
# health check uses that name.
    forward . 10.11.1.66 1.1.1.1 9.9.9.9 {
      policy sequential
      health_check 5s domain tswn.us.
    }
